<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i-1)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

However, the above assertion seems to incorrectly shift the indices. Let's correct it to properly reflect the behavior of the `add` method:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i-1));</code>

This correctly states that for every valid index from the insertion point to the old size of the list, the element at the current position is equal to the old element at the previous position.
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
